<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-World-of-Pokemon" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The World of Pokémon</title>

  <p>
    We live together with our friends and partners of the world - the Pokémon.
    In case your elementary school did not give you a proper education regarding these
    wonderful creatures, we will review the basics of our world.
  </p>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-Pokemon">
    <title>Pokémon</title>
    
    <p>
      Our world is filled with creatures called Pokémon ("pocket monsters").  There are 151
      of Pokémon in our region of Kanto, but some theorize that there may be other undiscovered
      species out there in other areas of the world.  

      <figure xml:id="fig-pokemon-ex">
      <sidebyside widths="45% 45%" valign="middle">
        <figure xml:id="fig-poke-1">
          <caption>Kangaskhan</caption>
          <image source="images/Kangaskhan.png">
            <shortdescription>Kangaskhan</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-poke-2">
          <caption>Magikarp</caption>
          <image source="images/Magikarp.png">
            <shortdescription>Margikarp</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </sidebyside>
      </figure>
    </p>

    <p>
      Pokémon are highly intelligent.  They can fully understand human speech although they
      communicate only by saying their species name in various tones.  All Pokémon in a species
      are very similar in appearance, and most people cannot distinguish one from another.
    </p>

    <p>
      Pokémon personalities vary greatly, and their role in our world is just as diverse.
      Most Pokémon live in the wild.  Some families have domesticated Pokémon in their homes.
      Some Pokémon even help us do our jobs.  For example, Chanseys frequently help our our
      Pokémon physicians and nurses in the Pokécenters.
      Some people even participate with Pokémon in our only sport - Pokémon battles.
    </p>

    <p>
      Many Pokémon have wondrous abilities.  Some can breath fire.  Some can generate
      electricity.  Some have the strength to move mountains.  
      Some have telekinetic abilities.  Some, like Magikarp, just flop
      around. 
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-Pokemon-Catching">
    <title>Pokémon-Catching</title>
    
    <p>
      Pokémon that do not live in the wild and become domesticated need to first be "caught."
      Catching Pokémon is simple.  All you need to do is throw a Pokéball at it.
      <figure xml:id="appeared">
        <caption>A wild Pokémon appearing</caption>
        <image source="images/Appeared.jpg" width="50%">
          <shortdescription>A wild Pokémon appearing</shortdescription>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </p>

    <p>
      Pokéballs are wonders of technology developed by Professor Oak.  These balls can house
      a Pokémon inside of it in a state of pure energy.  People can store and carry Pokémon
      wherever they go.
      <figure xml:id="pokeball">
        <caption>A Pokéball</caption>
        <image source="images/pokeball.jpg" width="50%">
          <shortdescription>A Pokéball</shortdescription>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </p>

    <p>
      However, there is a catch.  (Get it?)  Just because you throw a ball at a Pokémon,
      the catch may or may not be successful.  Think of it like a fish, such as 
      Magikarp or Goldeen nibbling on a hook.  They may or may not get set on the hook and
      able to be reeled in.  The likelihood of your catch being successful depends on a number
      of factors such as the toughness of the Pokémon or their current health or the type
      of Pokémon that you use.  If you have a successful catch, the Pokémon and the Pokéball
      used are connected, and that Pokémon is yours until you decide to release it.

      <figure xml:id="fig-catching">
      <sidebyside widths="45% 45%" valign="middle">
        <figure xml:id="fig-catch-fail">
          <caption>Pokémon catch failure</caption>
          <image source="images/BrokeFree.jpg">
            <shortdescription>A Pokémon breaking free</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-catch-success">
          <caption>Pokémon catch success</caption>
          <image source="images/Caught.png">
            <shortdescription>A Pokémon successfully being caught</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </sidebyside>
      </figure>
    </p>

    <p>
      Most Pokémon experts agree that if you treat your caught Pokémon with love and respect
      that they love their lives as domesticated Pokémon.  However, there are those out there, 
      such as Team Rocket, that see their caught Pokémon as tools.  Those Pokémon can develop
      similar selfish sentiments as their owners.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-Pokemon-Collecting">
    <title>Pokémon-Collecting</title>
    <p>
      Many people try to collect Pokémon.  In particular, many try to catch as many Pokémon
      species as possible.  They live by the code, "Gotta catch 'em all!"
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-Pokemon-Battling">
    <title>Pokémon Battling</title>
    <p>
      Our only sport in the world is Pokémon battling.  In a battle, Pokémon fight one-on-one
      using their various abilities.  A Pokémon will battle until it becomes exhausted and
      faints.  Usually, a battle consists of teams Pokémon.  A battle is over once all Pokémon
      on a team have fainted.

      <figure>
      <caption>Pokémon trainer challenge</caption>
      <image source="images/Battle1.png" width="50%">
        <shortdescription>Battle Begins</shortdescription>
      </image>
      </figure>
    </p>

    <p>
      Most experts agree that Pokémon like battling.  They share the competitive nature of
      their trainers and like to vie for victory.
    </p>

    <p>
      Humans act as trainers to Pokémon.  They help their Pokémon become stronger to compete
      better in battles.  Trainers travel the world, wanting to be the best Pokémon
      trainer - the best there ever was.

      <figure xml:id="pokemon-trainer-img">
        <caption>Pokémon Trainer</caption>
      <image source="images/Trainer.jpg" width="50%">
        <shortdescription>Pokémon Trainer</shortdescription>
      </image>
      </figure>
    </p>

    <p>
      Pokémon battling is a regulated sport with rules.  
    </p>
    
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            One of the most important rules is
            that a team can have at most six Pokémon on it.  Some trainers choose to have fewer
            than six on their team, for some reason, but it is allowed.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            When Pokémon battle, they take turns with whatever maneuver their trainer has
            instructed them to do.  For example, Trainer A may instruct their Pokémon to use
            the Fury Swipes move, which it will do.  Then Trainer B may instruct their 
            Pokémon to use the Rain Dance move.  Then Trainer A may instruct their Pokémon to
            use another move.  This goes on until one Pokémon faints.

            <figure>
              <caption>Pokémon taking a turn</caption>
            <image source="images/Battle2.jpg" width="50%">
              <shortdescription>Pokémon taking a turn.</shortdescription>
            </image>
            </figure>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            When a battle is over, the losing trainer must give the winner a portion of the
            any cash they are carrying.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Eight trainers have so much experience and earned such a reputation as trainer 
            that they lead their own Pokémon gym.  Trainers may challenge these gym leaders
            whenever they wish.  Winning a battle against a gym leader is a mark of great
            honor for a trainer and his Pokémon partners.  They are awarded a badge for 
            winning a against a gym leader.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Four individuals have such regional renown that they have formed as a group of 
            the nearly un-defeatable "Elite Four."  If a trainer were defeat all members of
            the Elite Four in battles, they are dubbed the Champion and are a Pokémon Master.

            <figure xml:id="weedle-evolution">
              <caption>The Elite Four</caption>
            <image source="images/Elite.png" width="50%">
              <shortdescription>Elite Four</shortdescription>
            </image>
            </figure>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            There are no age restrictions to participate as a trainer.  In fact, many ten-year-olds
            travel the world alone with their Pokémon pals as trainers.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      Some people, mainly those in Team Rocket, will use Pokémon to battle other than sport.
      They will use their Pokémons' abilities to commit crimes such as hijacking a radio
      station or commandeering a cruise ship.

      <figure xml:id="teamrocket">
        <caption>Team Rocket members</caption>
        <image source="images/TeamRocket.jpg" width="50%">
          <shortdescription>Team Rocket members</shortdescription>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-Pokemon-Evolution">
    <title>Pokémon Growth and Evolution</title>

    <p>
      Pokémon grow stronger through battle.
    </p>

    <p>
      Every Pokémon has statistics or "stats" that are measurements of its health and
      battling abilities.  For example, health points (or HP) measure a Pokémon's health,
      which decreases as it battles.  It will faint if it's HP becomes 0.  A Pokémon's
      Special Attack (or Sp. Att.) determines how powerful it's ability-based attacks are
      in battle.  The higher the number, the more it can decrease its opponent's HP in a turn.
    </p>

    <p>
      Despite theories of natural selection, evolution happens in a much different way.  
      When a Pokémon grows old enough and becomes strong enough through battling, it
      can evolve into a different species instantly.  For example, when a Weedle become strong
      enough, it will evolve into a Kakuna.  When a Kakuna becomes strong enough, it will
      evolve into a Beedrill.  So far, we have not seen a Beedrill evolve into anything 
      stronger.

      <figure>
      <caption>The Evolution of Weedle</caption>
      <image source="images/WeedleEvo.jpeg" width="50%">
        <shortdescription>Evolutions of Weedle</shortdescription>
      </image>
      </figure>
    </p>
  </subsection>

</section>